
{"id":"25de19af-fd43-41f1-902a-6530a2d72f50","object":"chat.completion","created":1786438898,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_018\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 3,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 0,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 5,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 11,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 0,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated repair optimization. We must output a JSON object with record_id and decisions. The decisions must include every mission exactly once. Fixed UAV assignment and mission order must be respected. The UAV assignment is already fixed per mission (uav_id in mission definition). Mission order is sequence_index: for each UAV, missions must be served in increasing sequence_index order. We also must respect release/due windows, route compatibility, capacity constraints (corridor, pad, charger), closures and overrides, and energy constraints. The baseline decision is provided, but we need to produce a new repair considering the intervention overrides.\n\nKey differences from baseline: There are intervention overrides:\n- initial_soc_overrides: UAV_02 initial_soc changed to 2.731181 kWh (baseline had 3.82163).\n- corridor_capacity_overrides: COR_01 capacity at slot 0 is set to 1 (default is 2). So only one UAV can use COR_01 at slot 0.\n- closed_corridor_slots: Many corridors have specific slots closed. This includes COR_05, COR_04, COR_06, COR_01, COR_10. We need to check each path's corridor usage against these closures. Also, COR_03 is closed for all slots except slot 5? Actually, list shows COR_03 closed at slots 0-4,6-23. So COR_03 is only open at slot 5. But that's a corridor ID used by PATH_06 (MISSION_03, UAV_02) with travel_slots=1 and offset 0. So MISSION_03's corridor COR_03 must be used at slot 5 only if it departs at slot such that its travel occurs at slot 5. For a path with travel_slots=1, it occupies exactly the depart_slot? Need to interpret: edge_segments have offset_slot 0, so travel occurs at departure slot? Usually, a path with travel_slots=2 occupies slots depart_slot and depart_slot+1? The edge_segments have offset_slot=0, so the corridor is used from depart_slot+offset_slot to depart_slot+offset_slot+duration_slots-1. For MISSION_03's PATH_06: travel_slots=1, duration_slots=1, offset_slot=0, so it uses slot depart_slot. Since COR_03 is only open at slot 5, MISSION_03 must depart at slot 5. Baseline had depart_slot=5, which matches.\n\nOther closures: \n- COR_04 closed all slots 0-23 (entirely closed). Paths using COR_04: PATH_05 (MISSION_02). Also possibly others? PATH_05 uses COR_04, duration_slots=2, offset_slot=0. Since COR_04 closed all day, PATH_05 is unavailable. So we cannot use PATH_05 for MISSION_02. Baseline used PATH_05 at depart_slot=0, which would occupy slots 0 and 1? But COR_04 closed at slots 0,1,... so it's not allowed. So MISSION_02 must choose a different path.\n- COR_05 closed at slots 2-23. So COR_05 is open only at slots 0 and 1. PATH_04 uses COR_05, travel_slots=2, duration_slots=2. So if we use PATH_04 for a mission, it must depart at slot 0 so that it occupies slots 0 and 1, which are open. If depart at 1, occupies 1 and 2; slot 2 closed. So only possible depart_slot=0 for PATH_04. But MISSION_02 release slot is 0, due slot 6, so depart_slot=0 possible. Baseline used PATH_05, which is now blocked. We have PATH_03 (COR_01), PATH_04 (COR_05), PATH_08 (COR_10). COR_01 has capacity override slot 0 capacity=1. Also COR_01 closed slots 2-23 (from closed_corridor_slots: COR_01 closed slots 2,3,...23). So COR_01 open only at slots 0 and 1. PATH_03 uses COR_01, travel_slots=2, duration_slots=2. So to avoid closure, depart must be slot 0 (occupying 0 and 1). But then it competes with other uses of COR_01 slot 0. CAP_01 slot 0 capacity =1. So only one mission can use COR_01 at slot 0. \n- COR_10 closed slots 2-23, open only 0,1. PATH_08 uses COR_10, travel_slots=2, duration_slots=2. So similarly must depart at 0 to occupy 0-1. \nSo for MISSION_02 departing VP_03 to VP_02, we have available paths:\n- PATH_03 (COR_01): requires depart slot 0 (occupies 0,1), and capacity at slot 0 limited to 1. It can be used if no other mission uses COR_01 at slot 0.\n- PATH_04 (COR_05): requires depart slot 0 (occupies 0,1). COR_05 open at 0,1, but COR_05 also closed slots 2-23 so need 0,1. But COR_05 capacity is default 2? No override, so 2.\n- PATH_08 (COR_10): requires depart slot 0 (occupies 0,1). COR_10 capacity 2.\nBut MISSION_05 is also assigned to UAV_01, also departing from VP_03 to VP_02 at release slot 0, due slot 2, using a path among its single option PATH_01 which uses COR_01. So MISSION_05 must use PATH_01 (COR_01) with travel_slots=2. Since release slot 0, due slot 2 means it must depart at 0 and arrive by slot 2? Actually due_slot 2, travel_slots=2, so if depart 0, arrival is at end of slot 1? Travel slots=2 means flight occupies depart_slot and depart_slot+1? The due_slot is the latest arrival slot? Typically, a mission must be completed (arrive) at or before due_slot. Start at depart_slot, then travel takes travel_slots slots, so arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? Or arrival at the start of next slot? But given due_slot 2, and travel 2 slots, it likely must depart at 0 to finish by slot 1 or 2. Let's interpret: If travel takes 2 slots, depart at slot 0, flight spans slots 0 and 1, arriving at end of slot 1 (ready for turnaround at start of slot 2?). The due_slot 2 might mean must arrive by end of slot 2? Usually due_slot is the slot by which service flight is completed, so arrival slot <= due_slot. If travel_slots=2, depart_slot=0, then the mission is in flight during slots 0 and 1, and arrives at the end of slot 1, so arrival slot = 1? Slot indexing: probably 0-indexed. So due_slot 2 means must finish by 2. So depart_slot 0 is fine. So MISSION_05 likely needs PATH_01, depart slot 0. BASELINE: MISSION_05 was held in baseline? Wait, baseline decisions show MISSION_05: held: false, depart_slot: 0, path_id: PATH_01, uav_id: UAV_01. So baseline had both MISSION_02 and MISSION_05 both departing VP_03 at slot 0. MISSION_02 used PATH_05 (COR_04) which is now closed. So now we must assign MISSION_02 a different path. MISSION_05 still uses COR_01, and must depart at 0. So COR_01 slot 0 is used by MISSION_05. However, COR_01 slot 0 capacity is 1 due to override. So only one UAV can use COR_01 at slot 0. Therefore MISSION_02 cannot use PATH_03 if MISSION_05 also uses COR_01 at slot 0. Could MISSION_05 delay its depart? Its due slot is 2, travel 2 slots, so if it departs at 1, it would occupy slots 1 and 2. But COR_01 closed at slot 2, so slot 2 is closed. So cannot depart at 1 because slot 2 closed. So MISSION_05 must depart at 0, using COR_01 slot 0-1. Therefore COR_01 slot 0 is taken by MISSION_05. So MISSION_02 cannot use PATH_03. So MISSION_02 must use either PATH_04 (COR_05) or PATH_08 (COR_10). Both require depart at 0 (to avoid closure). So MISSION_02 must depart at 0. But MISSION_02 and MISSION_05 both departing VP_03 at slot 0. Pad capacity at VP_03 is 2, so two simultaneous departures okay. Corridor usage: MISSION_05 uses COR_01 at 0-1; MISSION_02 uses one of the others. We need to check corridor capacities: COR_05 capacity 2, open 0-1; COR_10 capacity 2, open 0-1. So either works. We'll pick one that minimizes something. Also we must consider energy and other constraints.\n\nNow other missions:\n- MISSION_01: UAV_01, VP_02 to VP_05, release 3, due 20. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_07), PATH_09 (COR_06), PATH_10 (COR_02). Baseline used PATH_09 (COR_06) with depart_slot=3, travel_slots=2. COR_06 closures: closed slots 3-26 (list includes 3-23, 24-26? Closure listed up to 26, but horizon is 24 slots? Actually horizon_slots=24, so slots 0-23. The closure list includes slots 3,4,...22,23,24,25,26 - but 24+ are beyond horizon? Probably irrelevant, but if we depart at 3, then COR_06 would be used at slots 3 and 4? Travel_slots=2, offset 0, so occupies depart_slot and depart_slot+1 = 3 and 4. Both closed. So PATH_09 cannot be used at depart 3. Maybe can use at other depart slots? But COR_06 is closed for all slots from 3 onward, so if we want to use PATH_09, we need depart_slot <=? If depart_slot=0, then occupies slots 0 and 1, which are open? Check closures for COR_06: it says \"COR_06 closed at slots\": list includes 3,4,...,23,24,... So slots 0,1,2 are not listed, so open. So perhaps COR_06 is open at 0,1,2. To use PATH_09, we could depart at slot 0,1,2? If depart at 2, occupies 2 and 3 - but 3 closed. So depart at 2 uses slot 3 as second slot? duration_slots=2, so if depart at 2, slots 2 and 3. Slot 3 closed, so invalid. So depart_slot must be such that both slots are open. Since open slots 0,1,2, only possible depart_slot=0 or 1. depart_slot=0 uses slots 0,1; depart_slot=1 uses slots 1,2. Both open. So MISSION_01 could use PATH_09 with depart at 0 or 1, but release slot is 3, so can't depart before release. Therefore PATH_09 is unavailable because release 3 forces depart >=3, but any depart >=3 would involve slot 3+ which is closed. Thus PATH_09 is impossible. PATH_02 (COR_07): COR_07 closures? Not listed as closed in closed_corridor_slots. So COR_07 fully open. PATH_02 uses COR_07, travel_slots=2. So can depart at any slot >= release 3. PATH_10 (COR_02): COR_02 also not listed in closures? Not in closed_corridor_slots. So CO_02 open. So MISSION_01 can choose PATH_02 or PATH_10. Baseline used PATH_09 which is now impossible, so must change. Energy considerations: UAV_01 has initial SOC 9.62204 kWh, battery capacity 16.0, reserve 1.5. MISSION_05 must be served first (sequence_index 0 for UAV_01). Let's compute energy for MISSION_05: PATH_01 uses COR_01, distance 1.06 km, empty_energy_kwh_per_km = 0.1, payload 0.5 kg, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg = 0.026. Wind level: edge_segments shows wind_level 1, and UAV_01 wind_energy_multiplier_per_level = 0.11. So energy = (empty_energy_per_km * distance + payload_energy_per_km_kg * payload * distance) * (1 + wind_level * wind_multiplier). Empty energy: 0.1 * 1.06 = 0.106. Payload energy: 0.026 * 0.5 * 1.06 = 0.01378. Total base = 0.11978. Wind multiplier: 1 + 1 * 0.11 = 1.11. So energy = 0.11978 * 1.11 = 0.1329558, matches given energy 0.132956. Good. So MISSION_05 energy consumption = 0.132956 kWh. Then reserve check: after incoming deadhead (none) and service flight, SOC must remain >= reserve (1.5). Initial SOC 9.62204, minus 0.132956 = 9.489084, well above 1.5. So okay. Then turnaround at destination VP_02 takes 1 slot (turnaround_slots=1). Then post-service charge. Charge blocks can be 0-3. Charger capacity at VP_02 is 2 per slot. So we can charge. But note that MISSION_05 due_slot 2, so must arrive by end of slot 1 (if depart 0, travel 2, arrival slot 1?). Then turnaround slot: after arrival, there is a turnaround of 1 slot before it can start charging? Normative order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So turnaround occurs at destination after service flight and reserve check, before charge. Turnaround takes 1 slot. So if arrival is at end of slot X, turnaround occupies slot X+1? Probably: after flight arrives at end of slot, the UAV is available to start turnaround at next slot? Typically, if travel_slots=2, depart slot 0, flight uses slots 0 and 1. Arrival at end of slot 1. Turnaround would then occupy slot 2? The due_slot 2 likely means completed service flight (including reserve check) by slot 2? But turnaround is after reserve check, so it's not part of due constraint? The due slot applies to the mission's service flight completion (arrival?). Usually due slot is the deadline for the transport service. So turnaround and charging can happen after due slot. So MISSION_05 with due 2 must complete flight (arrival) by slot 2. If travel 2 slots, arrival at end of slot 1 (if depart 0) or end of slot 2 (if depart 1?). With depart 0, arrival at end of slot 1, satisfying due 2. So that's fine. Turnaround at slot 2. So UAV_01 will be at VP_02, turnaround slot 2, then can charge starting slot 3. So MISSION_05 charging would happen at VP_02 from slot 3 onward.\n\nNow MISSION_01: UAV_01 sequence_index 1, after MISSION_05. It starts from VP_02 (origin), going to VP_05. So after MISSION_05, UAV_01 is at VP_02, available after turnaround and any charging. MISSION_01 release 3, due 20. So we need a path from VP_02 to VP_05. Available: PATH_02 (COR_07, 2 slots, energy 0.113, wind 0) or PATH_10 (COR_02, 2 slots, energy 0.50172, wind 1). PATH_02 is much more energy efficient. We'll likely choose PATH_02. But we must check corridor availability. COR_07 open, no closures. So PATH_02 is good. Depart_slot must be >= release 3, and also must consider that UAV_01 must finish turnaround and necessary charging, and then travel. With MISSION_05 arriving end slot 1, turnaround slot 2, so UAV_01 ready to start next mission at earliest slot 3. Since release 3, that's perfect. So MISSION_01 can depart at 3. Baseline had depart_slot=3 for MISSION_01. So we can use PATH_02, depart 3, arriving end of slot 4 (travel_slots=2, depart 3, flight slots 3,4). Energy consumption: energy_kwh 0.113 (as given, wind 0). So after MISSION_05, SOC was 9.489084, then turnaround slot 2 (no energy consumed). Then before MISSION_01, we could charge at VP_02. Charge_kwh_per_block for UAV_01 = 1.2. Max blocks per mission = 3. But we need to ensure battery capacity not exceeded. SOC after MISSION_05 = 9.489084. If we charge 0 blocks, SOC 9.489084, after MISSION_01 consumption 0.113 = 9.376084, still above reserve 1.5. So no charge needed. But maybe we want to charge later for MISSION_04? After MISSION_01, UAV_01 is at VP_05, then MISSION_04 (sequence_index 2) from VP_05 to VP_04, release 11, due 23. So after MISSION_01, we will have turnaround at VP_05 (1 slot), then can charge there. We'll need energy for MISSION_04: PATH_07 energy 0.169331, wind 1. So not high. So likely no urgent need for early charging. So we can set charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_05 and MISSION_01. \n\nNow MISSION_04: UAV_01, from VP_05 to VP_04, release 11, due 23. Path only one: PATH_07 (COR_08) travel_slots=1, energy 0.169331. COR_08 closures? Not listed, so open. Depart_slot must be >= 11. MISSION_01 arrival at VP_05 end of slot 4 (depart 3, 2 slots). Then turnaround at VP_05 occupies slot 5. So UAV_01 ready from slot 6 onward. So we could depart as early as 6, but release 11, so must wait until 11. So depart at 11. Baseline had depart_slot=11. Energy: SOC before MISSION_04: after MISSION_01 SOC=9.376084, turnaround slot 5 at VP_05 (no consumption). Then we could charge at VP_05 from slot 6 to 10 (5 slots). But each charge block takes 1 slot? Charge time: \"charge_kwh_per_block\": 1.2, but each block likely takes 1 slot. max_charge_blocks=3 per mission (post-service charge). So we can charge for up to 3 blocks after MISSION_01 at VP_05, but the charge occurs during the post-service phase. The slots available: after turnaround at slot 5, we can begin charge at slot 6. If we do 3 blocks, that would occupy slots 6,7,8. Then UAV free at slot 9. Then we wait until 11 to depart. SOC after 3 blocks: 9.376084 + 3*1.2 = 9.376084+3.6=12.976084, but battery capacity is 16.0, so fine. Then MISSION_04 consumption 0.169331, SOC after = 12.806753. Reserve 1.5, ok. Could also do fewer blocks. Baseline had charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_01. So they did not charge. That would give SOC 9.376084 at MISSION_04, then after MISSION_04 9.20675. Both fine. We could choose any. But to optimize lexicographically: we have criteria: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. We need to decide if any mission must be held. The baseline held none. With the closure overrides, we might need to hold some missions if not feasible? Let's check feasibility of all missions with the closures.\n\nNow UAV_02 missions:\nUAV_02: start VP_03, initial SOC overridden to 2.731181 kWh. Battery capacity 22.0, reserve 2.5. Mission order: MISSION_02 (sequence 0) VP_03->VP_02, release 0, due 6. Then MISSION_03 (sequence 1) VP_02->VP_01, release 5, due 18. \nMISSION_02 path options: PATH_03 (COR_01), PATH_04 (COR_05), PATH_08 (COR_10). As discussed, all require depart at 0 to avoid closures. COR_01 taken by MISSION_05 at slot 0. So we must pick PATH_04 or PATH_08. Both have distance and energy: PATH_04: distance 0.9 km, energy 0.1359, max wind 0. PATH_08: distance 1.31 km, energy 0.19781, max wind 0. So PATH_04 is more energy efficient. We'll pick PATH_04 if no other constraints.\nBut PATH_04 uses COR_05. COR_05 closed slots 2-23, open 0-1. Depart at 0, occupies slots 0,1. That's fine. Capacity 2. So we can depart MISSION_02 at slot 0 using PATH_04. Then MISSION_05 (UAV_01) also departs at slot 0 using COR_01 (PATH_01). No corridor conflict since different corridors. Pad at VP_03 capacity 2, so both can depart simultaneously. So both missions feasible at slot 0.\n\nNow energy for MISSION_02: initial SOC 2.731181. PATH_04 energy 0.1359. So after service flight, SOC = 2.731181 - 0.1359 = 2.595281. Reserve 2.5, so 2.595281 > 2.5, barely above reserve! That's tight. So reserve check passes. After MISSION_02, turnaround at VP_02 (1 slot). MISSION_02 travel_slots=2, depart 0, arrival end slot 1. Turnaround slot 2. Due slot 6, so fine.\n\nNext MISSION_03: UAV_02 from VP_02 to VP_01, release 5, due 18. Only one path: PATH_06 (COR_03) travel_slots=1, energy 0.128652. But COR_03 closures: only open at slot 5 (as listed: closed 0-4,6-23). So MISSION_03 must depart at slot 5, so that travel occurs at slot 5 (since travel_slots=1). Baseline had depart_slot=5, so same. But we need to check if UAV_02 can be ready by slot 5. After MISSION_02, arrival end slot 1, turnaround slot 2, then UAV_02 free at VP_02 from slot 3 onward. It needs to depart at slot 5. So we can charge at VP_02 during slots 3,4 (and possibly 5? but depart at 5, so cannot charge during travel). We can do up to 3 charge blocks after MISSION_02. charge_kwh_per_block = 0.95 for UAV_02. Current SOC after MISSION_02: 2.595281. If we do 0 blocks, SOC stays 2.595281, then MISSION_03 energy 0.128652, SOC after = 2.466629, which is below reserve 2.5! 2.466629 < 2.5, so fails reserve after service flight. Reserve check is after service flight (before turnaround). So we must charge enough to ensure after MISSION_03 consumption, SOC >= reserve. Alternatively, we could charge after MISSION_02 but before MISSION_03. The post-service charge for MISSION_02 happens at VP_02 after its turnaround, before the next mission. So we can add charge_blocks for MISSION_02 to boost SOC. Let's calculate needed charge. Target: after MISSION_03, SOC >= 2.5. Starting from after MISSION_02 SOC = 2.595281. If we add C blocks of 0.95 each, then SOC before MISSION_03 = 2.595281 + 0.95*C. Must be <= 22 battery capacity. Then MISSION_03 energy = 0.128652. So after mission: 2.595281 + 0.95*C - 0.128652 >= 2.5. => 0.95*C >= 2.5 - 2.595281 + 0.128652 = 0.033371 => C >= 0.0351. So even C=0 gives 2.466629 < 2.5, so need at least 1 block. With C=1: 2.595281 + 0.95 = 3.545281; after mission: 3.545281 - 0.128652 = 3.416629 > 2.5, ok. So we need at least 1 charge block after MISSION_02. Can we do 1 block? Charging takes slots. MISSION_02 turnaround occupies slot 2. Then we can start charge at slot 3. 1 block takes slot 3. Then UAV free at slot 4. Then depart MISSION_03 at slot 5. So that works. So MISSION_02 charge_blocks = 1.\n\nBut we also need to check that charger capacity at VP_02 is not exceeded. Each charger can serve 1 UAV per slot? Charger_capacity_by_slot_default at VP_02 is 2, meaning 2 chargers, so can charge up to 2 UAVs simultaneously. At VP_02 during slots 3,4,5,... we will have UAV_02 charging (slot 3) and possibly others? UAV_01 after MISSION_05 might also charge at VP_02? MISSION_05 (UAV_01) arrives VP_02 end slot 1, turnaround slot 2, then could charge at VP_02. But we set charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_05, so no charging. So only UAV_02 charging at VP_02 slot 3. So capacity fine.\n\nAlso corridor capacity: COR_03 open only slot 5, capacity 2, so fine.\n\nNow check MISSION_01 and MISSION_04 for UAV_01: we already planned PATH_02 depart 3, no charge; MISSION_04 depart 11. But we must consider corridor closures for PATH_07 (COR_08). COR_08 not listed as closed, so open. So no issue.\n\nNow we must also ensure pad capacity at vertiports: VP_03 departure: UAV_01 MISSION_05 and UAV_02 MISSION_02 both depart at slot 0. Pad capacity VP_03 is 2, so okay. VP_02: later arrivals and departures. MISSION_05 arrival VP_02 end slot 1, occupies pad during approach? Pad capacity likely applies to takeoff/landing. But we'll assume it's within capacity.\n\nNow check MISSION_03: after MISSION_02, we depart at 5. MISSION_03 travel 1 slot, so uses slot 5, arrival at end slot 5? travel_slots=1, so arrives at end of slot 5. Turnaround at VP_01 slot 6. Due 18, fine. No further missions for UAV_02, so no need for charging after.\n\nNow all missions assigned, no holds. We need to make decisions accordingly. But we must confirm no other paths forcing conflicts. For UAV_02 MISSION_02, we choose PATH_04 with depart 0, charge_blocks 1. Energy check: initial SOC 2.731181 minus 0.1359 = 2.595281, reserve check passes. Then +0.95 = 3.545281, capacity 22 ok. For MISSION_03, PATH_06, depart 5, charge_blocks 0 (since it's last mission, no need). But after MISSION_03, we have turnaround at VP_01, then post-service charge could occur, but no later missions, so charge_blocks=0. Energy after MISSION_03: 3.545281 - 0.128652 = 3.416629, >2.5. So ok.\n\nNow check repair distance: sum of distances? Not required to output but our objective includes repair distance. We'll use PATH_04 (0.9 km) vs PATH_08 (1.31) vs PATH_03 (1.06 but unavailable). So choose PATH_04. For MISSION_01, PATH_02 (1.0 km) vs PATH_10 (4.0 km). Choose PATH_02. So total distance minimal.\n\nNow check weighted tardiness: all missions within due slots. MISSION_05 due 2, arrival end slot 1, fine. MISSION_02 due 6, arrival end slot 1, fine. MISSION_01 due 20, arrival end slot 4, fine. MISSION_03 due 18, arrival end slot 5, fine. MISSION_04 due 23, arrival end slot 11 (travel 1), fine. So no tardiness.\n\nNow \"delay plus charge wait\": that might include waiting for release or due to charging? Possibly any unnecessary waiting. We have some waiting for release for MISSION_04: ready at slot 6 but release 11, so 5 slots idle. That's okay, can't avoid. For MISSION_01: ready slot 3, departure 3, no wait. MISSION_03: ready after charge at slot 4? After charge, UAV_02 free at slot 4? With charge block at slot 3, UAV_02 free at start of slot 4? But MISSION_03 must depart at slot 5 due to corridor open. So wait 1 slot. That's needed. So okay.\n\nNow we must produce JSON. The record_id is \"CA12_CORE_018\". The decisions: for each mission, assign uav_id, held false, path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks as determined.\n\nMission order: MISSION_01, MISSION_02, MISSION_03, MISSION_04, MISSION_05. But sequence_index assignment is per UAV: UAV_01 missions: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_01 (seq 1), MISSION_04 (seq 2). UAV_02 missions: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1). We must list all five decisions in any order, but each mission exactly once.\n\nNow verify corridor closures and capacities again. For MISSION_05 PATH_01 uses COR_01 slots 0-1. COR_01 capacity override slot 0=1. That's okay since only one mission uses COR_01 at slot 0 (MISSION_05). MISSION_02 PATH_04 uses COR_05 slots 0-1. COR_05 capacity 2, open 0-1. MISSION_01 PATH_02 uses COR_07 slots 3-4? depart 3, travel_slots=2, so uses slot 3 and 4. COR_07 open, capacity 2. MISSION_04 PATH_07 uses COR_08 slot 11? depart 11, travel_slots=1, uses slot 11. COR_08 open. MISSION_03 PATH_06 uses COR_03 slot 5. COR_03 open only slot 5, capacity 2. So all good.\n\nCheck pad capacity: VP_03 slot 0 departures: 2 UAVs, pad cap 2 ok. VP_02 slot 1 arrival UAV_01, slot 1 arrival UAV_02? Actually MISSION_02: depart 0, travel 2, arrival VP_02 end slot 1. MISSION_05: depart 0, travel 2, arrival VP_02 end slot 1. So two arrivals at VP_02 at end of slot 1. Pad capacity VP_02 is 2, so fine. VP_02 later: depart MISSION_01 at slot 3 from VP_02; so that's a departure. Pad capacity 2, so ok. VP_05: arrival MISSION_01 at end slot 4, then later departure MISSION_04 at slot 11. VP_04 arrival MISSION_04 end slot 11. VP_01 arrival MISSION_03 end slot 5. All capacities seem fine.\n\nNow, do we need to consider incoming deadhead? For all missions, incoming_deadhead is null, so no consumption.\n\nThus the repair is feasible.\n\nNow output JSON structure: \n{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_018\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":3,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_04\",\"depart_slot\":0,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav